WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:03.704
A robot can complete a movement and still fail the task.

2
00:00:03.874 --> 00:00:08.170
CoMET monitors what should be true, before, during, and after each action.

3
00:00:10.500 --> 00:00:13.217
The flow starts with one successful demonstration.

4
00:00:13.387 --> 00:00:19.366
It becomes a structured description, then a condition-annotated behaviour tree, which coordinates online
monitoring.

5
00:00:22.500 --> 00:00:28.328
Here, the robot moves towards the basketball, grasps it, transports it, and releases it into the hoop.

6
00:00:28.498 --> 00:00:30.031
These are this task’s stages.

7
00:00:33.500 --> 00:00:39.967
Side, front, and wrist views are combined with gripper state, held-object identity, and pose information.

8
00:00:40.137 --> 00:00:44.967
The model produces a goal, named objects, and ordered waypoint descriptions.

9
00:00:46.500 --> 00:00:52.259
A reusable catalogue defines conditions such as held, released, inside, and aligned with.

10
00:00:52.429 --> 00:00:57.920
The six available primitives are move, grasp, transport, place, release, and push.

11
00:00:59.500 --> 00:01:03.854
Tasks do not all use the same primitives, or the same number of stages.

12
00:01:04.024 --> 00:01:07.739
Basketball uses move, grasp, transport, and release.

13
00:01:07.909 --> 00:01:13.482
A pushing task can use move and push, while shelf placement can also include place.

14
00:01:13.652 --> 00:01:18.052
Primitives may repeat; objects, conditions, and goals change with the task.

15
00:01:19.500 --> 00:01:25.885
The generator assigns one primitive per waypoint and grounds its conditions in task-specific objects.

16
00:01:26.055 --> 00:01:31.779
A reviewer checks completeness and consistency; fixed rules attach the applicable detectors.

17
00:01:32.900 --> 00:01:40.388
Each stage follows the same monitoring structure: check preconditions, execute with applicable monitors,
then check postconditions.

18
00:01:40.558 --> 00:01:44.692
During transport, the basketball must already be held and remain held.

19
00:01:46.900 --> 00:01:52.728
At checkpoints, a vision-language model evaluates the active conditions using three camera views.

20
00:01:52.898 --> 00:01:58.250
One call combines the previous stage’s postconditions with the next stage’s preconditions.

21
00:01:59.900 --> 00:02:06.077
Robot-state detectors watch for collisions during motion, and grasp loss during transport and place.

22
00:02:06.247 --> 00:02:09.799
Position and orientation are checked at waypoint completion.

23
00:02:09.969 --> 00:02:13.208
A push stage does not require maintaining a grasp.

24
00:02:14.900 --> 00:02:20.740
In this disturbed basketball recording, the grasp succeeds, but the ball is lost during transport.

25
00:02:20.910 --> 00:02:23.545
The task fails before the intended release.

26
00:02:25.900 --> 00:02:30.126
A grasp-loss or collision candidate triggers a targeted visual check.

27
00:02:30.296 --> 00:02:35.764
The model receives before-and-after views and recent detector signals to confirm or reject the event.

28
00:02:35.934 --> 00:02:38.094
Pose deviations are checked directly.

29
00:02:39.900 --> 00:02:43.708
If the robot never grasps the ball, there may be no grasp-loss signal.

30
00:02:43.878 --> 00:02:48.267
The visual condition check can still flag that the required object is not held.

31
00:02:51.900 --> 00:02:56.323
CoMET reports the stage, violated condition, and supporting explanation.

32
00:02:56.493 --> 00:03:02.194
Detecting a violation is distinct from naming its cause; benchmark failure labels use mapping rules.

33
00:03:03.300 --> 00:03:10.208
Across seventy-eight tasks, the supplied study reports eighty-three point three percent binary F1 for CoMET.

34
00:03:10.378 --> 00:03:16.740
Direct prompting reaches fifty-four point four; adding the same detectors and confirmation reaches
eighty-two point eight.

35
00:03:16.910 --> 00:03:19.186
CoMET also improves macro F1.

36
00:03:20.300 --> 00:03:23.539
Failure detection is stronger than exact diagnosis.

37
00:03:23.709 --> 00:03:30.861
Collision types and sequence violations remain challenging, and detector-triggered visual confirmation adds
computational cost.

38
00:03:31.967 --> 00:03:35.949
The idea is reusable, but the monitoring plan is task-specific.

39
00:03:36.119 --> 00:03:41.448
Demonstrate success. Define what must hold. Monitor execution. Explain deviations.
